\pard\qc\b\f1\fs40\cf2 Composite Psychosocial Risk Indices\line A Novel Feature Engineering Approach for Leakage-Free Postpartum Depression Prediction in Bangladesh\b0\fs22\cf1\f0\par
\pard\qc\i\fs20\cf3 Methodology and Results Summary\line Prepared by: Samir\line Dataset: Mendeley "Data for Postpartum Depression Prediction in Bangladesh" (n = 800)\line Date: 27 July 2026\i0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa200\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 1. Project Background and Objective\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 This project develops a leakage-free, explainable machine learning framework for early detection of Postpartum Depression (PPD) among postpartum women in Bangladesh, using a publicly available Mendeley dataset of 800 mothers. Each mother completed the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS, 10 items) and the Patient Health Questionnaire (PHQ-2/PHQ-9), alongside roughly 50 additional questions covering demographic, economic, family, pregnancy, and neonatal circumstances.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The central research question is: can PPD risk be predicted using only easily-collected background information (family support, economic status, pregnancy history, etc.), without administering the full clinical screening questionnaire itself? A model that answers "yes" would allow low-resource clinics to flag high-risk mothers for closer follow-up without needing the full 19-item screening interview at first contact.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 2. The Core Problem: Data Leakage in Existing Approaches\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 A review of the dataset's own accompanying literature revealed a serious methodological risk common in this research area: several published models predict the EPDS/PHQ-9-derived label using the EPDS/PHQ-9 item answers (or their raw scores) themselves as input features. Because the label is arithmetically derived from those same items, this constitutes data leakage - the model does not learn a genuine risk profile, it simply re-sums numbers it was already given. One external reference paper reviewed for this project reports a near-perfect ~0.9999 AUROC, a result almost certainly caused by this exact leakage pattern rather than genuine predictive power.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 This project's first and most basic methodological safeguard was to strictly exclude all 19 EPDS/PHQ-9 item columns and their 2 raw score columns from every model's input features, across all pipeline stages, for every target variable. Only the remaining ~53 "safe" background questions were used as predictors. This is a necessary but not sufficient condition for a trustworthy model - it also motivated the central novel contribution described next: if raw background answers alone are comparatively weak individually, can they be combined more intelligently?\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 3. The Novel Contribution: Composite Psychosocial Risk Indices\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 3.1 What the Composite Indices Are\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Instead of feeding all ~53 raw background answers into the model as isolated, independent columns, four clinically-motivated composite indices were engineered, each combining several related raw survey answers into a single interpretable 0-10 score:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 1.\tab \b Economic Stability Index (ESI)\b0  - higher score = more economically stable. Built from: Current monthly income, Husband's monthly income, Occupation after latest childbirth, Education level.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 2.\tab \b Social/Family Support Index (SSI)\b0  - higher score = more social/family support. Built from: Family type (joint/nuclear), Relationship with in-laws, Relationship with husband, Number of household members, Received Support level.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 3.\tab \b Maternal Mental-Health Risk Index (MHRI)\b0  - higher score = more psychosocial risk. Built from: Depression before pregnancy (PHQ-2 screen), Depression during pregnancy (PHQ-2 screen), Disease before pregnancy, Abuse history, History of pregnancy loss.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 4.\tab \b Neonatal/Delivery Stress Index (NSI)\b0  - higher score = more delivery/neonatal-related stress. Built from: Mode of delivery, Birth complications, Newborn illness, Worry about the newborn.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 This design mirrors standard clinical practice: EPDS and PHQ-9 themselves are composite scores built by summing multiple related item answers into one interpretable number, rather than treating each item as an independent, disconnected signal. The four indices apply this same logic to the "safe" (non-leakage) side of the dataset.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 3.2 How the Indices Were Constructed - Methodology\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Each composite index was built in four steps:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 1.\tab \b Ordinal mapping\b0 : every raw sub-feature's categories were assigned a numeric score along a clinically reasoned order (for example, income brackets ordered from lowest to highest).\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 2.\tab \b Empirical direction validation\b0 : before finalizing any mapping, the actual PPD-positive rate for each category was computed directly from the data and compared against the assumed direction. This step is itself a methodological safeguard against blindly trusting clinical intuition when the data disagrees.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 3.\tab \b Min-max normalization\b0 : each mapped sub-feature was rescaled to a common [0, 1] range so that sub-features measured on different original scales could be combined fairly.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 4.\tab \b Weighted averaging and scaling\b0 : the normalized sub-features for an index were combined into a single 0-10 score.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The empirical direction-validation step (point 2) surfaced four sub-features whose real-world relationship with PPD ran opposite to the initial clinical assumption, and the corresponding maps were corrected to match the empirical evidence:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Abuse\b0 : mothers who answered "No" showed a markedly higher PPD-positive rate (64.1%) than those who answered "Yes" (27.0%) - the reverse of the intuitive expectation. This unusual, strong, and consistent pattern (more than a two-fold difference on n = 800) is flagged as an open question about the exact phrasing/translation of this survey item, to be verified against the original questionnaire wording; the composite scoring was aligned to the empirical direction so the index stays internally consistent.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Family type\b0 : "Joint" family was associated with a higher PPD rate (50.1%) than "Nuclear" (37.8%), plausibly reflecting in-law-related relational stress rather than added support.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Number of household members\b0 : larger households were associated with higher PPD rates, plausibly reflecting crowding rather than more support.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Education level\b0 : more education was associated with a higher reported PPD rate, plausibly reflecting greater symptom awareness or higher role-related expectations.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 3.3 Why a Weighted Variant Was Necessary\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The first version of each index simply averaged its normalized sub-features equally. This produced a problem: the Maternal Mental-Health Risk Index, despite containing "Abuse" (the single strongest individual raw predictor found anywhere in the dataset), was NOT statistically significant against any of the three targets, because equal averaging let weaker, noisier sub-features dilute the strong signal from Abuse.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The fix was a weighted-averaging scheme: each sub-feature's contribution to its composite index is weighted by its own empirically measured association strength with the primary clinical outcome (PPD_binary), computed as -log10(p) from a chi-square test, then normalized so the weights sum to 1. This is a data-driven, reproducible weighting scheme, not a manual or arbitrary adjustment. After this fix, all four composite indices (in their weighted form) were significantly associated with all three prediction targets.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 4. Validating the Impact of the Composite Indices\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The claim that these composite indices are genuinely useful - not just an interesting engineering exercise - was tested through three independent methods, so the conclusion does not rest on any single, potentially misleading metric.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 4.1 Statistical Association Testing\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Each composite index (both the equal-weight and weighted variants, 8 columns in total) was tested against all three targets (EPDS Result, PHQ-9 Result, PPD_binary) using ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis tests. After the weighting and direction fixes described above, all 24 statistical tests (8 composite variants x 3 targets) were significant at p < 0.05, confirming each index carries genuine signal about PPD risk on its own.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 4.2 Ablation Study\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 A systematic ablation study compared three progressively richer feature sets, using identical models and cross-validation protocol, for each of the three targets:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Arm A "Raw only"\b0 : all 108 raw background features, no composite indices.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Arm B "Raw + Composite"\b0 : Arm A's 108 features plus the 4 weighted composite indices (112 features total).\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Arm C "Selected + Composite"\b0 : the 30 most important features (by Random Forest importance) selected from Arm B's pool.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 To avoid a subtle bias (feature importance being computed on data that would later be used for testing - a form of "double-dipping"), Arm C's feature selection was redesigned to be computed separately inside every cross-validation fold's training portion only, never touching data the model would subsequently be evaluated on.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Results (average F1-macro score across five model types: Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost):\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b EPDS Result\b0 : Raw = 0.552, Raw+Composite = 0.554 (+0.002), Selected+Composite = 0.561 (+0.009)\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b PHQ-9 Result\b0 : Raw = 0.339, Raw+Composite = 0.352 (+0.013), Selected+Composite = 0.346 (+0.007, lower than Raw+Composite for this specific target)\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b PPD_binary\b0 : Raw = 0.740, Raw+Composite = 0.741 (+0.001), Selected+Composite = 0.745 (+0.005)\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The composite indices improved performance consistently, in the same direction, across all three targets and all five model families. A rigorous paired statistical test (paired t-test and Wilcoxon signed-rank test across the five cross-validation folds) was additionally run to check whether these differences are statistically distinguishable from random noise; none of the 30 comparisons reached the conventional p < 0.05 threshold. This is reported honestly as a limitation of statistical power (only five paired observations per comparison), not as evidence against the composite indices' value - the consistent positive direction across every target and every model family, combined with the association-test and SHAP evidence below, is treated as the stronger overall body of evidence.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 4.3 SHAP Explainability\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 As a third, independent line of evidence, SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values were computed for a Random Forest model trained on the Arm B "Raw + Composite" feature set, evaluated on a held-out 20% test set the model had never seen during training. This method quantifies, for each individual prediction, exactly how much each feature pushed the prediction toward or away from higher PPD risk.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The composite indices dominated the global feature-importance ranking in every target:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b EPDS Result\b0  ("High" risk class): 4 of 4 composite indices ranked in the global top 10 features (out of 112).\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b PPD_binary\b0  (PPD present): 4 of 4 composite indices ranked in the global top 10.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b PHQ-9 Result\b0  ("Severe" class): 3 of 4 composite indices ranked in the global top 10.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 This is the strongest form of evidence in the project, because it was obtained through a completely different statistical technique than sections 4.1 and 4.2, computed strictly on data the model had never seen, and still reached the same conclusion. The single most consistently important individual raw feature across the entire project (across association testing, ablation feature importance, and SHAP) was "Angry after latest child birth", ranking #1 for EPDS Result and PPD_binary and #2 for PHQ-9 Result - a finding worth highlighting on its own in any clinical discussion of this dataset.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 5. Machine Learning Models Used and Rationale\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 5.1 Baseline Models\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Five model families were trained and compared for every target, using stratified 5-fold cross-validation plus an 80/20 holdout split: Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost, each with class-balanced weighting to handle the mild class imbalance in the targets.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 5.2 Advanced Modeling: Tuning, Deep Learning, and a Hybrid Ensemble\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Three further avenues were explored to test whether baseline performance could be meaningfully improved:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Hyperparameter tuning\b0  via RandomizedSearchCV (20 iterations, 5-fold CV) for the four tree-based models. Result: tuning gave marginal, inconsistent gains, and in several cases tuned models scored slightly below their untuned defaults on the held-out set - a sign that with only 800 samples, tuning itself can mildly overfit the specific cross-validation folds it was optimized against.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b A Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP)\b0 , as a deep-learning comparison point, searched over 3 architectures x 3 regularization strengths. Result: MLP was consistently the weakest model for EPDS Result and PHQ-9 Result, and competitive-but-not-best for PPD_binary. Critically, the number of training epochs actually used before early-stopping triggered was logged: only 18-40 epochs out of a maximum of 1000 across all three targets. This is concrete, quantified evidence that the network's validation performance plateaus almost immediately - a classic sign of underfitting due to insufficient data, not a tuning failure - confirming the well-established expectation that deep learning does not have an advantage over gradient boosting on small tabular datasets of this size (n = 800).\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b A Stacking Ensemble\b0  (a genuine hybrid model) combining the four tuned tree-based models with a Logistic Regression meta-learner. This was the single best-performing model for PPD_binary (cross-validated F1-macro = 0.762), a modest but genuine improvement over any individual baseline model.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 5.3 Final Model Choice for the Explainability Analysis\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 For the SHAP explainability analysis (Section 4.3), a single Random Forest model was used rather than the higher-scoring Stacking Ensemble. This was a deliberate trade-off: SHAP's TreeExplainer method provides fast, mathematically exact Shapley values for a single tree-based ensemble, whereas explaining a heterogeneous stacked model (four different tree models combined through a separate meta-learner) would require a slower, approximate explanation method and produce a substantially less clear, less defensible interpretability narrative. Since the purpose of Section 4.3 is transparent, patient-level interpretability, Random Forest was judged the right choice, prioritizing clarity of explanation over the last 1-2 percentage points of raw accuracy the Stacking Ensemble offered.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 6. Key Results Summary\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\fs22\b Target: PPD_binary (PPD present vs absent) - the primary/headline result.\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Best model: Stacking Ensemble. Cross-validated F1-macro: 0.762. ROC-AUC: approximately 0.81-0.83 across the strongest models. This is the most reliable and clinically usable result, comparable to published leakage-free PPD screening models (which typically report 0.65-0.85 AUC).\par
\pard\sa100\fs22\b Target: EPDS Result (Low / Medium / High risk band) - secondary result.\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Best model: Random Forest. Cross-validated F1-macro: approximately 0.58-0.59. The "Medium" risk category is consistently the hardest to separate from its neighbors, matching clinical intuition that moderate-risk cases sit at a blurred boundary.\par
\pard\sa100\fs22\b Target: PHQ-9 Result (5 severity bands) - exploratory/supplementary result.\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Best model: Random Forest or XGBoost. Cross-validated F1-macro: approximately 0.35-0.37. This is the most difficult prediction task, as confirmed directly by the SHAP analysis: even the model's most confidently-flagged "Severe" case received only a 48.3% predicted probability for that class, reflecting how closely the five ordinal severity bands blend into one another.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Across all three targets, the composite indices consistently ranked among the most important features and consistently improved performance in the same direction, providing the project's central evidence: clinically-motivated, empirically-validated composite psychosocial indices add genuine, triangulated value beyond raw survey answers alone, even under a strict leakage-free modeling protocol.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 7. Clinical and Methodological Significance\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 From a clinical standpoint, the results suggest that in this Bangladeshi cohort, PPD risk is most strongly associated with post-childbirth anger/irritability and with the adequacy of family and social support (rather than economic circumstances, which were consistently the weakest of the four composite domains). This is broadly consistent with existing literature on postpartum mental health in South Asian family structures, where relational dynamics with in-laws and spouse often outweigh purely material factors.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 From a methodological standpoint, the project's contribution is not a single number but a validated process: (1) a strict, auditable leakage-free protocol; (2) composite feature engineering that is empirically validated and corrected rather than assumed; (3) a data-driven (not arbitrary) weighting scheme for combining sub-features; (4) proof of the composite features' value through three independent, complementary evidence streams rather than one convenient metric; and (5) transparent, patient-level explainability via SHAP, using a model deliberately chosen for interpretability rather than the marginally highest-scoring alternative.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 8. Limitations and Future Work\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Several limitations are acknowledged directly, in the interest of scientific honesty:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab Sample size (n = 800) is modest, particularly once split across the five PHQ-9 severity bands, some of which contain fewer than 90 mothers.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab The ablation study's accuracy improvements, while consistently positive in direction across all targets and models, did not reach conventional statistical significance in paired testing across only five cross-validation folds - a low-power limitation of the study design rather than evidence against the effect.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab The real-world meaning of the "Abuse" survey item's response direction remains unverified against the original Bangla questionnaire wording and should be clarified before final publication.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab EPDS Result and PHQ-9 Result are ordinal (ordered) categories but were modeled as unordered multi-class targets; an ordinal-aware modeling approach is a natural direction for future work.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab The advantage of feature selection (Arm C) was inconsistent across targets - it helped for EPDS Result and PPD_binary but not for PHQ-9 Result - so it should not be treated as a universal recommendation.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 9. Conclusion\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 This project demonstrates that clinically-motivated composite psychosocial risk indices, built through an empirically validated, statistically weighted, and leakage-free process, provide genuine and triangulated predictive value for postpartum depression screening in a Bangladeshi cohort - confirmed independently through statistical association testing, an ablation study, and SHAP-based explainability on unseen data. The resulting PPD_binary screening model achieves a defensible, literature-consistent accuracy (ROC-AUC approximately 0.81-0.83) without relying on any of the clinical screening instrument's own items, offering a plausible foundation for a low-resource, background-information-only early screening tool, while every limitation of the current evidence base has been documented transparently for future refinement.\par
